  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe Erds minimum overlap problem requires finding a step function h on [0, 2] integrable to 1 that minimizes the maximum overlap with its shifts. The standard optimization approaches used may get stuck in local minima. Our approach uses a multi-resolution strategy starting from coarse grids, properly enforced constraints, sequence generation, and optimization using quadratic programming (QP) and projected gradient methods. The key novelty is the explicit separation of the integral constraint from other feasibility checks and the use of 1-step innovations to modify candidate functions while ensuring the integral remains 1. This approach uses projection-based optimization with exact gradient information, leveraging all available shifts for gradient updates.\n\nMathematical specification:\nVariables: h \u2208 \u211d^(n) \u2013 a step function vector.\nConstraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i, \u2211h_i = n/2 (enforced after each update).\nObjective: Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 || h \u2297 (1 - h_{shifted}) || \u221e dx\n\nWe reformulate using auxiliary variables and set up a constrained optimization problem. The integral constraint is separated and enforced via re-normalization after each update. The overlap integral is computed for all integer shifts using discrete correlation.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Initialization:\n- Start at resolution n=32 with a basic pulse function.\n- Use the provided `initial_h_values` (n=560) only when fallback is allowed.\n\n2. Multi-resolution phase (phases 1-4):\nFor each resolution level with length n:\n  1. Generate a parent sequence at level 0 by coarsening up to n/2\n  2. Apply integral normalization to ensure \u2211h = n/2 * dx\n  3. Setup optimization function and gradient with respect to all shifts\n  4. Use projected gradient descent with two damping strategies for long steps\n  5. After each iteration, check greedy local improvements\n  6. Ensure overlap evaluation is performed for all lags\n\n3. Optimization control:\n- Terminate early if overlap improvement falls below tolerance\n- Monitor solver stability with constraint violations < 1e-4\n- Fall back to parent solutions at specified intervals\n\n4. Validation:\n- Evaluate full correlation for all 2*n-1 lags\n- Compute the score: C\u2085 = max(lags) * dx\n\n5. Down-sampling strategy:\nAdaptive grid coarsening: levels determined by sqrt(n) / level_factor^iteration\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n1. Initial resolution: Start at 32 samples, then adjust up/down based on improvement\n2. Damping factor: Start at 0.99, increasing to 0.999 on stagnation\n3. Projection decisions: Full projection if constraint violation > 0.01, otherwise targeted\n4. Learning rate strategy:\u516b\u5927\u4ee5\u6765\u3069\u306e\u518d\u8a66\u884c\u308215\u30b9\u30c6\u30c3\u30d7\u3001\u521d\u671f75%\u3001then adaptive warmup\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Primary optimization: 600 seconds\n- Validation: 50 seconds per solution\n- Coarsening: 5 steps, each 20 seconds\n- Backup: Return fallback after 2 mistaken attempts\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Check: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h * dx \u2248 1 (within 1e-4)\n2. Score: np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') * dx\n3. Validation must complete before return\n\nSolver parameters for CVXOPT:\n- 'maxiters': 1000\n- 'abstol': 1e-8\n- 'reltol': 1e-8",
